      },
      "components": [
        {
          "id": "body",
          "type": "text",
          "enabled": true,
          "order": 0,
          "props": {
            "body": "The realtime layer is where backends go to die. You start with a WebSocket, add a heartbeat, then a reconnect handler, then a message queue, then a Redis pub/sub, then a presence tracker, then a sticky-session load balancer, then a Kafka topic for replay. Six months later, three engineers are full-time on it and your p99 latency is whatever the last on-call wants it to be. Pulse replaces all of that with one HTTP+WebSocket API and a typed SDK that handles reconnects, ordering, and replay automatically. The full API surface is described by an AISP-compatible OpenAPI 4 schema that round-trips between prose, code, and generated docs with under 2% ambiguity. You can paste a customer's bug report into our docs site and the navigator will jump straight to the endpoint that explains it. We have seven regions with anycast routing, three layers of edge caching, and a 99.99% uptime SLA backed by a public status page that doesn't lie. Ten dollars a month covers a million events. Free tier covers your hackathon. The pricing page has no asterisks."
          }
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "heading": "Why Pulse",
        "subheading": "The realtime layer, finally as boring as a database"
      }
    },
